Must prove copying for infringement;
No UDR in 2D or surface decoration, colour or texture;
Components not visible in normal use can be protected by UDR; 
Computer generated designs can also be protected by UDR (mentioned in s.214);
2D computer generation is a design document for an article; 
No UDR in e.g. connectors as these are features of shape or configuration which enable an article to be connected to another article so that either article may perform its function; 
Licences of right available in the last 5 years of UDR protection;
Qualification requirement: residence – those who are habitually resident or economically active in the UK or certain other qualifying countries are able to claim UK unregistered design right, irrespective of their nationality;
	No such requirement for registered designs, nor for community unregistered designs;
	Nationality restriction for UDR removed Oct. 2014.

1. A design shall be considered to be new if no identical design has been made available to the public:
	(a) in the case of an unregistered Community design, before the date on which the design for which 	   	      protection is claimed has first been made available to the public;
	(b) in the case of a registered Community design, before the date of filing of the application for 	  	  	      registration of the design for which protection is claimed, or, if priority is claimed, the date of priority.
